  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1028 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a split flow pattern develops and persists.\nWhile another upper disturbance is progged to move onto the \ncentral Plains by Sat, limited moisture return suggests precip \nchances will be sparse, and this wave isn't quite in phase with a \nsouthern stream low over AZ which lessens the large-scale forcing.\nThe NBM PoPs are low, just 10-15%, but with little else in the \nforecast right now it's at least a time frame to monitor.\n\nTemps will be on the cool side Thurs-Sun, and while a freeze is\nstill unlikely there may be some nights that are cool enough for \nfrost, especially Thurs night into Fri AM as the presence of sfc \nhigh pressure results in good radiational cooling conditions \n(clear skies, light winds). Highs during this time period will \ngenerally be in the upper 50s and 60s, while lows will range from \nmid/upper 30s to mid 40s. A slight warming trend is anticipated \ninto the following week.\n\nPrecipitation may return by the middle of next week, depending on\nthe evolution of the closed low over the SW US (and perhaps an \nadditional wave moving through the southern jet stream). However, \nguidance is often too quick with these closed upper lows, and \ndeterministic models hint at a Rex Block developing over the \nwestern US early next week (upper ridge poleward of upper lows), \nand this blocking pattern would also favor a slower evolution. All\ntold, rain occurring next week is far from a sure thing.",
